 'excerpt' => 'Companies sell shares to raise money without borrowing it. Here is why that suits some companies, and what they give up.',
 'seo_description' => 'Why companies sell shares to the public: raising money without debt, rewarding early owners, and the trade-offs of sharing ownership.',
 'body' => '<p>Selling shares is one of the main ways a company raises money. Understanding why companies do it helps you see where your money goes when you invest.</p>
<h2>To raise money to grow</h2>
<p>A company that wants to build a new factory, open new branches or launch a new product needs cash. It can borrow, but loans must be repaid with interest. When it sells shares instead, the money does not have to be paid back. In return, the new shareholders become part-owners and share in future profits.</p>
<h2>To let early owners and investors cash out</h2>
<p>Founders and early investors often own large stakes. Selling some of their shares to the public lets them turn part of their ownership into cash, while the company carries on.</p>
<h2>To build trust and visibility</h2>
<p>Companies that sell shares publicly must publish regular financial reports and follow rules set by the regulators. Many companies find that this openness makes banks, suppliers and customers trust them more.</p>
<h2>What the company gives up</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Part of the ownership.</strong> Existing owners own a smaller percentage after new shares are issued.</li><li><strong>Privacy.</strong> Public companies must disclose a lot of information.</li><li><strong>Pressure.</strong> Shareholders expect results, and they can vote against directors.</li></ul>
<h2>Who gets the money?</h2>
<p>It depends on the kind of sale. When a company issues brand new shares, the money goes to the company. When existing shareholders sell their shares to other investors, the money goes to the seller. Our guide to the primary and secondary markets explains the difference.</p>'
