 'excerpt' => 'Three ways to put money to work, with very different promises. Here is how a share, a bond and a savings account compare.',
 'seo_description' => 'Share vs bond vs savings account: what you own or are owed, how each one pays you, and how much risk each one carries.',
 'body' => '<p>A savings account, a bond and a share can all grow your money. They work in very different ways, and they carry different risks.</p>
<h2>A savings account: you lend to a bank</h2>
<p>When you save money in a bank, you are lending it to the bank. The bank pays you interest and lets you withdraw your money. Deposits in licensed banks are protected by the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC) up to a set limit. Returns are usually modest, but the money is easy to reach.</p>
<h2>A bond: you lend for a fixed period</h2>
<p>A bond is a loan to a government or a company. In return, the borrower promises to pay you interest at set times and to return your money on a fixed date. The FGN Savings Bond is an example: it is issued by the Federal Government through the Debt Management Office and pays interest every quarter. Bond returns are more predictable than share returns, but your money is tied up for the period.</p>
<h2>A share: you become a part-owner</h2>
<p>A share is not a loan. You own part of a company. You may receive dividends, and the price of your share can rise, but nothing is promised. If the company does badly, the price can fall and dividends can stop.</p>
<h2>Side by side</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Savings account:</strong> you are a lender to a bank. Return: interest. Risk: low.</li><li><strong>Bond:</strong> you are a lender to a government or company. Return: fixed interest, then your money back. Risk: low to medium.</li><li><strong>Share:</strong> you are an owner. Return: dividends and price changes. Risk: higher, with no guarantees.</li></ul>
<p>Many people hold a mix. Which one suits you depends on when you need the money and how much ups and downs you can accept.</p>'
