 'excerpt' => 'Nobody sets a share price by decree. It comes from buyers and sellers agreeing on a price, and many things influence what they agree on.',
 'seo_description' => 'Who decides what a share is worth? How buyers and sellers set share prices, what drives a company\'s value, and the difference between price and value.',
 'body' => '<p>The price you see for a share is not set by the company, the exchange or the government. It is set by people buying and selling.</p>
<h2>Price comes from agreement</h2>
<p>A share\'s price is simply the last price at which a buyer and a seller agreed to trade. If more people want to buy than sell, buyers have to offer more and the price tends to rise. If more want to sell than buy, the price tends to fall.</p>
<h2>What buyers and sellers think about</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Profits.</strong> How much money the company earns, and whether that is growing.</li><li><strong>Dividends.</strong> How much the company pays out to shareholders.</li><li><strong>Debt.</strong> A company with heavy debt is riskier.</li><li><strong>The outlook.</strong> Competition, the economy, interest rates and the exchange rate all matter.</li></ul>
<h2>Price is not the same as value</h2>
<p>The price is what the market says today. Value is what you think the company is really worth. Investors try to find companies whose value is higher than their price. They are often wrong, which is why prices move.</p>
<h2>Market capitalisation</h2>
<p>Multiply the share price by the number of shares the company has issued and you get its market capitalisation, the market\'s current total value for the company. A low price per share does not mean a small or cheap company. The number of shares matters too.</p>
<h2>How easily you can trade</h2>
<p>Some shares trade many times a day and others rarely. When few people trade a share, it can be harder to sell at the price you expect. This is called liquidity, and it is worth checking before you buy.</p>'
