 'title' => 'Primary market and secondary market: who gets your money?',
 'excerpt' => 'When you buy a share, your money does not always go to the company. It depends on whether you buy in the primary or secondary market.',
 'seo_description' => 'Primary vs secondary market explained: who receives your money when you buy new shares in an offer and when you buy shares on the exchange.',
 'body' => '<p>When you buy a share, you might assume your money goes to the company. Sometimes it does, and sometimes it does not. The difference is the market you buy in.</p>
<h2>The primary market: new shares</h2>
<p>The primary market is where new shares are created and sold for the first time. This happens in initial public offerings (IPOs), public offers and rights issues. When you buy in the primary market, your money goes to the company, less the costs of the offer. The company uses it to grow, pay off debt or fund projects.</p>
<h2>The secondary market: existing shares</h2>
<p>The secondary market is where investors buy and sell shares that already exist. This is what happens day to day on the Nigerian Exchange. When you buy a share on the exchange, the money goes to the investor who sold it to you, not to the company.</p>
<h2>An example</h2>
<ul><li>A company issues 100 million new shares in a public offer. Investors who buy them pay the company.</li><li>Later, one of those investors sells 1,000 shares on the exchange. A new buyer pays that investor.</li></ul>
<h2>Why the secondary market still matters to companies</h2>
<p>The company does not receive money from secondary trades, but the market matters to it. Investors are more willing to buy new shares if they know they can sell them later. The price in the secondary market also shows what investors think of the company.</p>
<h2>What this means for you</h2>
<p>Most of what you do as an everyday investor happens in the secondary market. Offers in the primary market come up less often, and you should only take part in offers that the SEC has approved.</p>'
